# MVC Routing Architecture - MANDATORY

## Core Principle

**Routes route. Views structure. Partials contain.**

Each layer has ONE job. Never mix concerns.

## Router Files - STRICT REQUIREMENTS

### What Routes Must Do
- Register URL endpoints
- Pass minimal data to views
- Handle request/response only

### What Routes Must NEVER Do
- Contain business logic
- Build HTML strings
- Filter/transform data
- Load external data
- Include complex calculations
- Contain pagination logic

## The Clean Pattern

### ✅ CORRECT: Minimal Route (routes/index.js, routes/brand.js)

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const router = express.Router();

router.get('/', function(req, res, next) {
  res.render('index', { 
    title: 'kjon · life', 
    description: 'd4 · implimentation' 
  });
});

module.exports = router;
```

**Why this is correct:**
- Route only routes
- Minimal data passed (title, description for meta tags)
- No logic
- No HTML generation
- Single responsibility

### ❌ FORBIDDEN: Complex Route (legacy kjon.js, v-0-0-3.js)

```javascript
// 🚫 DO NOT DO THIS
router.get('/', function(req, res) {
  // ❌ Loading data in route
  const quotesData = require('../public/quotes.json');
  const maximsData = require('../public/maxims.json');
  
  // ❌ Business logic in route
  const jjQuotes = quotes.filter(quote => quote.author === "k jon");
  const otherQuotes = quotes.filter(quote => quote.author !== "k jon");
  
  // ❌ Building HTML in route
  article.content = pageQuotes.map(quote => `
    <div class="box">
      <blockquote>${quote.quote}</blockquote>
    </div>
  `).join('');
  
  // ❌ Pagination logic in route
  const currentPage = parseInt(req.query.page) || 1;
  const startIndex = (currentPage - 1) * 3;
  
  // ❌ Complex data structure passed to view
  res.render('kjon', { 
    header: { articlesData }, 
    articlesData, 
    title, 
    description 
  });
});
```

**Why this is wrong:**
- Business logic mixed with routing
- HTML strings generated in JavaScript
- Hard to test
- Hard to maintain
- Violates separation of concerns
- Cannot reuse logic
- Difficult to debug

## View Files - STRICT REQUIREMENTS

### What Views Must Do
- Define page structure (HTML skeleton)
- Include partials for content
- Use semantic HTML (`<header>`, `<main>`, `<footer>`)
- Keep structure minimal and readable

### What Views Must NEVER Do
- Contain actual content (use partials)
- Include complex logic
- Have inline styles (use SCSS)
- Build HTML dynamically (delegate to partials)

## The Clean Pattern

### ✅ CORRECT: Structural View (views/index.ejs, views/brand.ejs)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <%- include('partials/head') %>
  </head>
  
  <body>
    <header>
      <%# include('partials/navigation') %>
    </header>
    <main>
      <!-- the intro section goes here -->
      <%- include('partials/intro') %>
      <!-- the purpose section goes here -->
      <%- include('partials/purpose') %>
      <!-- the cards section goes here -->
      <%# include('partials/cards') %>
    </main>
    <footer>
      <!-- the socials go here -->
      <%- include('partials/social') %>
      <!-- any scripts go here -->
      <%- include('partials/scripts') %>
    </footer>
  </body>
</html>
```

**Why this is correct:**
- Pure structure
- Human-readable comments
- Clear semantic HTML
- All content delegated to partials
- Easy to scan and understand
- Easy to modify (uncomment to enable sections)

### ❌ FORBIDDEN: Complex View

```html
<!-- 🚫 DO NOT DO THIS -->
<main>
  <% if (articles && articles.length > 0) { %>
    <% articles.forEach(article => { %>
      <div class="article">
        <h2><%= article.title %></h2>
        <% if (article.content) { %>
          <%- article.content %>
        <% } else { %>
          <p>No content available</p>
        <% } %>
      </div>
    <% }); %>
  <% } %>
</main>
```

**Why this is wrong:**
- Business logic in view (checking arrays, looping)
- Content generation in view
- Hard to test
- Difficult to reuse
- Mixed concerns

## Partial Files - REQUIREMENTS

### What Partials Must Do
- Contain actual content and markup
- Be self-contained (don't depend on parent view)
- Use semantic class names
- Handle their own structure

### What Partials Must NEVER Do
- Depend on variables from parent (unless explicitly passed)
- Contain routing logic
- Load external data (data comes from route)

## The Clean Pattern

### ✅ CORRECT: Self-Contained Partial (partials/purpose.ejs)

```html
<section id="purpose">
  <div class="purpose-container">
    <div class="text-container">
      <h2>We should talk!</h2>
      <p>
        <span class="phrase">We build LLM systems...</span>
        <span class="phrase">We ground enterprise LLMs...</span>
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="button-group">
      <button class="button-primary" onclick="window.open('https://zcal.co/jonhwilliams')">
        Let's talk
      </button>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
```

**Why this is correct:**
- Self-contained content
- Semantic HTML
- Semantic class names
- No dependencies
- Easy to include anywhere

## Middleware Registration (app.js)

### REQUIRED Pattern

```javascript
// Import route files
var indexRouter = require('./routes/index');
var brandRouter = require('./routes/brand');

// Register routes with URL paths
app.use('/', indexRouter);
app.use('/brand', brandRouter);
```

**Rules:**
- One `app.use()` per route file
- URL path matches route purpose
- Keep registration order logical
- Group related routes together

## Directory Structure

### REQUIRED Organization

```
routes/
  ├── index.js          # Home page route
  ├── brand.js          # Brand palette route
  └── colorplay.js      # Color playground route

views/
  ├── index.ejs         # Home page structure
  ├── brand.ejs         # Brand page structure
  └── partials/
      ├── head.ejs      # <head> content
      ├── intro.ejs     # Intro section
      ├── purpose.ejs   # Purpose section
      └── social.ejs    # Social links
```

**Rules:**
- Route name matches view name (`brand.js` → `brand.ejs`)
- Partials in `partials/` subdirectory
- One route file per page
- One view file per route

## Request Flow

```
User visits URL
     ↓
http://localhost:3000/brand
     ↓
app.js: app.use('/brand', brandRouter)
     ↓
routes/brand.js: router.get('/')
     ↓
res.render('brand', { title, description })
     ↓
views/brand.ejs (structure)
     ↓
Includes partials (content):
  - partials/head.ejs
  - partials/brand_palette.ejs
  - partials/social.ejs
     ↓
HTML sent to browser
```

## Data Flow

### Minimal Data Pattern

```javascript
// Route passes minimal data
res.render('brand', { 
  title: 'Page Title',           // For <title> tag
  description: 'Page description' // For meta description
});
```

### IF Data Required (Future Pattern)

When dynamic data is needed:

```javascript
// 1. Load in route (keep simple)
const items = getItems(); // Service layer, not in route

// 2. Pass to view
res.render('page', { items });

// 3. Partial iterates
// In partial:
<% items.forEach(item => { %>
  <div class="item"><%= item.name %></div>
<% }); %>
```

**Rules:**
- Load via service layer (not inline in route)
- Pass clean, prepared data
- Let partial handle display logic
- Keep route minimal

## EJS Include Syntax

### Active Include
```ejs
<%- include('partials/head') %>
```
Use `<%-` to include and render HTML

### Commented Include
```ejs
<%# include('partials/navigation') %>
```
Use `<%#` to temporarily disable a section

### Output Variable
```ejs
<title><%= title %></title>
```
Use `<%=` to output escaped content

## Adding a New Page

Follow this checklist:

### 1. Create Route File
```javascript
// routes/newpage.js
const express = require('express');
const router = express.Router();

router.get('/', function(req, res) {
  res.render('newpage', { 
    title: 'New Page', 
    description: 'Description' 
  });
});

module.exports = router;
```

### 2. Register in app.js
```javascript
var newpageRouter = require('./routes/newpage');
app.use('/newpage', newpageRouter);
```

### 3. Create View File
```html
<!-- views/newpage.ejs -->
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <%- include('partials/head') %>
  </head>
  <body>
    <main>
      <%- include('partials/newpage_content') %>
    </main>
    <footer>
      <%- include('partials/social') %>
      <%- include('partials/scripts') %>
    </footer>
  </body>
</html>
```

### 4. Create Content Partial
```html
<!-- views/partials/newpage_content.ejs -->
<section id="newpage">
  <div class="newpage-container">
    <!-- Your content here -->
  </div>
</section>
```

### 5. Add SCSS (if needed)
```scss
// src/scss/pages/_newpage.scss
#newpage {
  .newpage-container {
    // Styles
  }
}

// src/scss/main.scss
@use 'pages/newpage';
```

## Testing Routes

### Verify Route Registration
```bash
# Start server
pnpm start

# Test in browser
open http://localhost:3000/newpage
```

### Check Route Loading
```javascript
// In app.js, routes should load without errors
var newpageRouter = require('./routes/newpage'); // Should not throw
```

### Debug Route Issues

**Route not found (404)?**
- Check `app.use()` registration in app.js
- Verify route file exports router: `module.exports = router;`
- Check URL path matches registration

**Template not found?**
- Check view name matches: `res.render('brand')` → `views/brand.ejs`
- Verify file exists in `views/` directory

**Partial not found?**
- Check path is relative to `views/`: `include('partials/head')`
- Verify partial file exists

## Common Mistakes

### ❌ Wrong: Logic in Routes
```javascript
router.get('/', (req, res) => {
  const filteredData = data.filter(x => x.active); // Business logic
  res.render('page', { filteredData });
});
```

### ✅ Right: Clean Routes
```javascript
router.get('/', (req, res) => {
  res.render('page', { title: 'Page' });
});
```

### ❌ Wrong: Content in Views
```html
<main>
  <h1>Welcome to kjon.life</h1>
  <p>Long paragraph of content...</p>
</main>
```

### ✅ Right: Structure in Views
```html
<main>
  <%- include('partials/welcome') %>
</main>
```

### ❌ Wrong: HTML Strings in Routes
```javascript
const html = `<div>${content}</div>`;
res.render('page', { html });
```

### ✅ Right: Content in Partials
```javascript
res.render('page', { title: 'Page' });
// Content lives in partial, not route
```

## Primary Example: index.ejs Pattern

This is the gold standard pattern to follow:

**routes/index.js:**
```javascript
const express = require('express');
const router = express.Router();

router.get('/', function(req, res, next) {
  res.render('index', { 
    title: 'kjon · life', 
    description: 'd4 · implimentation' 
  });
});

module.exports = router;
```

**views/index.ejs:**
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <%- include('partials/head') %>
  </head>
  
  <body>
    <header>
      <%# include('partials/navigation') %>
    </header>
    <main>
      <%- include('partials/intro') %>
      <%- include('partials/purpose') %>
      <%# include('partials/cards') %>
    </main>
    <footer>
      <%- include('partials/social') %>
      <%- include('partials/scripts') %>
    </footer>
  </body>
</html>
```

**Key Characteristics:**
- Route: 9 lines, no logic
- View: Pure structure, human-readable
- Partials: Self-contained content
- Clear separation: routing → structure → content
- Easy to modify: comment/uncomment sections
- Scalable: Add new partials without touching route

## Quality Checklist

Before committing route changes:

- [ ] Route file has no business logic
- [ ] Route file has no HTML string building
- [ ] Route passes only minimal data (title, description)
- [ ] View file only contains structure and includes
- [ ] View file has semantic HTML tags
- [ ] View file has human-readable comments
- [ ] Content lives in partials, not in view
- [ ] Partials use semantic class names
- [ ] Route name matches view name
- [ ] URL registration in app.js is clear

## Benefits of This Pattern

1. **Readability**: Anyone can understand the flow
2. **Maintainability**: Easy to modify one layer without affecting others
3. **Testability**: Each layer can be tested independently
4. **Reusability**: Partials can be shared across pages
5. **Scalability**: Add pages without complexity growth
6. **Debuggability**: Clear where to look when something breaks
7. **Collaboration**: Multiple developers can work on different layers

## Remember

> "If your route file is more than 10 lines, you're doing it wrong."

Routes route. Views structure. Partials contain.

Keep it simple. Keep it clean. Keep it separated.